{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"99543f84e4a59ae4","type":"group","x":-800,"y":620,"width":2680,"height":1580,"label":"Understanding of cardinality"},
		{"id":"b692c1ee3a760f65","type":"text","text":"**What is Cardinality?**\n\n* **cardinality** defines the uniqueness and relationships within data \n* It measures either how many distinct values exists in a column.\n* How entities connect to each other across tables.\n\n\nLet's understand by taking an example:\n\n1. ***Cardinality in columns:(distinct values exists in a column)***\n\t1. It does mean how many distinct values exists in that column.\n\t2. CustomerId cardinality: `3` because IDs {1,2,3} are distinct\n\t3. CustomerName cardinality: `3` because names {Alice, Bob, Charlie} are distinct\n\t4.  We can say **high cardinality - lots of unique values(eg: customerID)** and **low cardinality - few unique values (eg: gender column with just Male/Female).**\n\n| Customer ID | Customer Name |\n| ----------- | ------------- |\n| 1           | Alice         |\n| 2           | Bob           |\n| 3           | Charlie       |\n| 4           | Alice         |\n2. ***Cardinality in relationships (how tables connect):*** \n\t\t1. In relational database, cardinality describes how rows in one table relate to rows in another table.\n\n\t***Example:*** \n\t\tWe have 2 tables *customer* and *orders* table.\n\t\t\t- *Customer → Orders = **One-to-Many**\n\t\t\t    - One Customer can have many Orders.\n\t        - Order → Customer = ***Many-to-One***\n\t\t\t    - *Many Orders can belong to one Customer.*\n\n\n**Quick Analogy:**\n1. *within a table* - \"how many different values are in this columns?\"\n2. *Across tables* - \"how many rows in one table match with rows in the other table?\"\n\n\n\n","x":-720,"y":680,"width":1020,"height":600},
		{"id":"3262176597125823","type":"text","text":"**Why Cardinality?**\n\nThe WHY of cardinality comes down to *control, performance, and accuracy* in data systems.\n*  It impacts how efficiently you can query data\n* how accurately you can model business relationship.\n* how effectively you can scale systems.\n\n***Core performance benefits:***\n1. Query optimisation: \n\t\t1. understanding cardinality enables databases engines to choose the most efficient execution plans, join strategies and indexing approaches.\n\t\t2. poor cardinality assumptions leads to slow, resource-intensive queries that can cripple system performance.\n2. Storage Efficiency:\n\t1. cardinality helps determine optimal data storage patterns, reducing redundancy and improving retrieval speed.\n\t2. businesses that depend on fast data access see significant performance gains when cardinality is properly configured.\n\n","x":-720,"y":1410,"width":1020,"height":670},
		{"id":"8cf5ab9bc7959fef","x":640,"y":680,"width":1020,"height":600,"type":"text","text":"**How is Cardinality?**\n\nThe HOW of cardinality implementation involves specific calculation methods, measurement techniques and practical tools that data engineers use to analyse and optimise data relationships and uniqueness.\n\n***Calculating column cardinality:***\n\n*Direct Counting:* The most straightforward method is using SQL queries to count distinct values:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT column_name) FROM table_name;\n```\n\tThis gives exact cardinality score for any column\n\n*Cardinality Ratio:* Calculate cardinality as a percentage by dividing distinct values by total rows:\n\n```sql\nCardinality Ratio = (COUNT(DISTINCT values) / COUNT(total rows)) * 100\n```\n\n### ***Statistical Measures***\n\n1. *Uniqueness Ratio*: how close values are to unique (For PK detection)\n2. *Null Proportion:* missing values checking \n3. *Entropy:* randomness vs categorical distribution.\n4. *Percent numeric/alphabetic/alphanumeric:* format hints.\n5. *Cardinality Ratio:* direct measure of diversity.\n\n\n\n\n\n"}
	],
